     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "Raju hung a bar magnet on a thread and saw it turn to point north-south.\n\"That is how a compass works,\" said his teacher."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "compass",
     "attract",
     "repel"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "A freely hanging magnet comes to rest pointing roughly north and south. The end pointing north is the north pole. A compass has a magnetic needle that shows direction.\nUnlike poles attract: north and south pull each other. Like poles repel: north and north push apart.\nA magnet's force acts even without touching, so it is a force at a distance."
    }
